>
	<p>
		I'm <strong>Elvis Tata Tanghang</strong>, a researcher in Electrical & Computer Engineering at Carnegie Mellon University (CMU). My work focuses on Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR), Natural Language Processing (NLP), and large language models (LLMs)—with a particular emphasis on low-resource and African languages.
	</p>

	<p>
		<strong>Neural Ground Truth (NGT)</strong> was born out of a desire to bridge the gap between academic rigor and intuitive understanding. Often, research papers are dense and difficult to unpack. Here, I break down complex architectures, training methodologies, and novel algorithms into digestible, visual, and code-backed explainers.
	</p>

	<h3>What to expect</h3>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Foundations:</strong> Deep dives into Attention, Tokenization, and decoding strategies.</li>
		<li><strong>LLMs & Speech:</strong> Architectural comparisons and efficiency improvements.</li>
		<li><strong>Low-Resource NLP:</strong> Challenges and solutions for languages with limited training data.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		Whether you're an AI researcher, a software engineer, or just curious about how modern machine learning models work under the hood, I hope you find these notes useful.
	</p>

	<p>
		You can find more about my academic background on my <a href="https://tanettech.github.io/">personal website</a> or connect with me on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/elvis-tata">LinkedIn</a>.
	</p>
</Layout>
